WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.589
Welcome to Asylum.

2
00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:03.093
Take this one slowly.

3
00:00:03.093 --> 00:00:06.888
By the end of the video you will have a
clear picture

4
00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:10.325
of what this app is for, and you will be
able to

5
00:00:10.325 --> 00:00:13.046
finish one real piece of work with it.

6
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.337
You do not need to be an expert.

7
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:17.342
You do not need to know Git.

8
00:00:17.342 --> 00:00:21.137
Every term gets explained the first time
it turns up.

9
00:00:22.637 --> 00:00:26.151
Here is the whole idea in its simplest
form.

10
00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:30.942
Imagine you ask three people to build the
same small bridge.

11
00:00:30.942 --> 00:00:36.052
If all three reach into one pile of
blocks, their hands collide,

12
00:00:36.052 --> 00:00:39.885
and afterwards nobody can tell who changed
what.

13
00:00:39.885 --> 00:00:44.517
So you give each person their own table,
their own copy of

14
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:47.871
the blocks, and the same instruction card.

15
00:00:47.871 --> 00:00:52.901
When they finish, you compare the bridges
and you keep the best

16
00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:53.859
one.

17
00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:59.449
Asylum does exactly that, with coding
assistants and the files in your

18
00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:00.407
project.

19
00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:04.779
Three words carry the entire app.

20
00:01:04.779 --> 00:01:09.565
A project is your box of blocks, which in
practice is a

21
00:01:09.565 --> 00:01:12.350
folder of code on your computer.

22
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:18.267
A task is the instruction card, written in
ordinary language, like a

23
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:21.487
note you would leave for a colleague.

24
00:01:21.487 --> 00:01:26.447
A run is one assistant's attempt at that
task, at its own

25
00:01:26.447 --> 00:01:27.492
table.

26
00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:32.452
Give one task to three assistants and you
get three runs.

27
00:01:32.452 --> 00:01:34.105
Project, task, run.

28
00:01:34.105 --> 00:01:37.934
Everything else hangs off those three
words.

29
00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:42.185
The product is one loop, repeated.

30
00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:43.722
You open a project.

31
00:01:43.722 --> 00:01:48.172
You describe what should be true once the
work is done.

32
00:01:48.172 --> 00:01:52.299
One or more assistants go away and work
separately.

33
00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:54.483
You compare what came back.

34
00:01:54.483 --> 00:01:58.609
Then you keep the best one, or you send a
note back

35
00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:01.684
and let a promising attempt try again.

36
00:02:01.684 --> 00:02:06.457
Every screen in this app exists either to
help you see that

37
00:02:06.457 --> 00:02:09.127
loop, or to make it safer to run.

38
00:02:10.627 --> 00:02:12.317
This works at any level.

39
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:16.752
If you are new to programming, use one
assistant for your first

40
00:02:16.752 --> 00:02:20.131
task and read the plain summary of what
changed.

41
00:02:20.131 --> 00:02:24.707
If you have been writing code for twenty
years, the exact branch,

42
00:02:24.707 --> 00:02:29.001
the folder on disk, the terminal output,
and the line by line

43
00:02:29.001 --> 00:02:32.310
difference are all right there in front of
you.

44
00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.774
It is the same workflow either way.

45
00:02:34.774 --> 00:02:38.434
You are only choosing how far down you
want to look.

46
00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:44.478
The first time you open Asylum it runs a
short check before

47
00:02:44.478 --> 00:02:45.864
letting you start.

48
00:02:45.864 --> 00:02:50.177
It looks for Git, which is the tool that
keeps a history

49
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:51.640
of changes to code.

50
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.876
It looks for at least one coding assistant
installed on this machine

51
00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:59.572
that you have already signed in to.

52
00:02:59.572 --> 00:03:04.038
And it checks that your project's own
tools actually work.

53
00:03:04.038 --> 00:03:06.964
Installed means the command was found.

54
00:03:06.964 --> 00:03:09.968
Verified means it completed a real run.

55
00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:14.126
Green here saves you from confusing
failures later on.

56
00:03:15.626 --> 00:03:19.988
This is the Tasks board, and it is where
you will spend

57
00:03:19.988 --> 00:03:21.891
nearly all of your time.

58
00:03:21.891 --> 00:03:26.491
Down the left are your projects and the
tasks inside them.

59
00:03:26.491 --> 00:03:31.487
Across the top is the composer, which is
where you describe new

60
00:03:31.487 --> 00:03:32.438
work.

61
00:03:32.438 --> 00:03:37.514
Underneath, every attempt appears as its
own card, showing which

62
00:03:37.514 --> 00:03:41.796
assistant made it, how long it took, and
how it ended.

63
00:03:41.796 --> 00:03:45.365
Nothing important is hidden behind a
summary.

64
00:03:46.865 --> 00:03:50.804
Now the part most people get wrong at the
start.

65
00:03:50.804 --> 00:03:53.923
Do not describe the steps you imagine.

66
00:03:53.923 --> 00:03:58.108
Describe the result you want to be true
afterwards.

67
00:03:58.108 --> 00:04:03.196
Say what should change, name any rule that
must not break, and

68
00:04:03.196 --> 00:04:07.136
include something you can check when it is
done.

69
00:04:07.136 --> 00:04:13.045
Here I am asking for clearer validation
messages, tests for two specific

70
00:04:13.045 --> 00:04:17.148
bad inputs, and no change to the public
interface.

71
00:04:17.148 --> 00:04:22.072
Small and checkable beats large and vague
every single time.

72
00:04:23.572 --> 00:04:27.550
Below the box you pick which assistants
get the job.

73
00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.381
For your very first task, choose one.

74
00:04:30.381 --> 00:04:35.354
Watch it work, read what it changed, and
get comfortable with the

75
00:04:35.354 --> 00:04:36.731
shape of the loop.

76
00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:41.704
Later, when a problem has more than one
sensible answer, pick two

77
00:04:41.704 --> 00:04:42.622
or three.

78
00:04:42.622 --> 00:04:47.135
Each one works alone and cannot see the
others, so what you

79
00:04:47.135 --> 00:04:52.491
get back are genuinely independent
attempts rather than three versions

80
00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:53.409
of one idea.

81
00:04:54.909 --> 00:04:59.992
Because that choice comes up constantly,
Asylum lets you save it.

82
00:04:59.992 --> 00:05:01.947
A duel is two assistants.

83
00:05:01.947 --> 00:05:03.277
A triad is three.

84
00:05:03.277 --> 00:05:07.109
A swarm is a larger group, with a cap on
how many

85
00:05:07.109 --> 00:05:09.064
start at the same moment.

86
00:05:09.064 --> 00:05:13.600
They are just named selections, and you
can make your own.

87
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.354
There is also a limit on how many runs may
go at

88
00:05:17.354 --> 00:05:22.672
once across the whole app, which protects
both your machine and your

89
00:05:22.672 --> 00:05:23.923
usage allowance.

90
00:05:25.423 --> 00:05:29.867
When you start the task, Asylum does the
setup for you.

91
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:34.958
Each assistant is given its own branch and
its own private copy

92
00:05:34.958 --> 00:05:36.735
of the project folder.

93
00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:40.694
Then each one is launched with your
instructions.

94
00:05:40.694 --> 00:05:45.461
From this moment they are all working at
the same time, and

95
00:05:45.461 --> 00:05:50.067
not one of them can disturb the others, or
the folder you

96
00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:51.441
actually work in.

97
00:05:52.941 --> 00:05:58.455
This is the part worth understanding
properly, because it is what makes

98
00:05:58.455 --> 00:06:00.086
everything else safe.

99
00:06:00.086 --> 00:06:02.882
A branch is a named line of changes.

100
00:06:02.882 --> 00:06:07.697
A worktree is a second, separate copy of
your project on disk,

101
00:06:07.697 --> 00:06:10.415
checked out from that same history.

102
00:06:10.415 --> 00:06:14.221
That is the separate table from the bridge
story.

103
00:06:14.221 --> 00:06:18.959
Three assistants can edit the very same
file at the very same

104
00:06:18.959 --> 00:06:23.308
second, because each one is touching its
own copy of it.

105
00:06:23.308 --> 00:06:26.259
Your original folder is never in play.

106
00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:32.328
Each card shows two things that sound
similar and are not.

107
00:06:32.328 --> 00:06:34.533
Status is about the process.

108
00:06:34.533 --> 00:06:38.392
Queued, running, succeeded, failed, or
cancelled.

109
00:06:38.392 --> 00:06:42.803
Activity is about what the assistant is
doing right now.

110
00:06:42.803 --> 00:06:45.323
Working, blocked, done, or idle.

111
00:06:45.323 --> 00:06:49.970
Succeeded only means the program finished
without an error.

112
00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.042
It does not mean the change is correct.

113
00:06:53.042 --> 00:06:58.003
Hold on to that distinction, because it is
the one that catches

114
00:06:58.003 --> 00:06:58.949
people out.

115
00:07:00.449 --> 00:07:03.551
Orange, blocked, is the one to watch for.

116
00:07:03.551 --> 00:07:08.695
It means the assistant has stopped, asked
a question, and is waiting

117
00:07:08.695 --> 00:07:10.360
on an answer from you.

118
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:15.126
Open that run and you see the whole
conversation, exactly as if

119
00:07:15.126 --> 00:07:17.623
you were sitting at its terminal.

120
00:07:17.623 --> 00:07:19.741
Answer it and it carries on.

121
00:07:19.741 --> 00:07:23.751
If it has gone off in the wrong direction,
cancel it.

122
00:07:23.751 --> 00:07:28.215
Its files are kept either way, so nothing
you might want is

123
00:07:28.215 --> 00:07:29.122
thrown away.

124
00:07:30.622 --> 00:07:33.605
Once an attempt finishes, open Review.

125
00:07:33.605 --> 00:07:37.764
What you are looking at is a diff, which
just means a

126
00:07:37.764 --> 00:07:38.706
comparison.

127
00:07:38.706 --> 00:07:40.511
Green lines were added.

128
00:07:40.511 --> 00:07:42.552
Red lines were taken away.

129
00:07:42.552 --> 00:07:47.026
Grey lines did not change and are only
there for context.

130
00:07:47.026 --> 00:07:50.793
You can read it as one column, or as two
side by

131
00:07:50.793 --> 00:07:51.735
side.

132
00:07:51.735 --> 00:07:53.304
Ask three questions.

133
00:07:53.304 --> 00:07:55.423
Did the right files change.

134
00:07:55.423 --> 00:07:56.993
Is anything missing.

135
00:07:56.993 --> 00:07:59.819
Did something unrelated get touched.

136
00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:02.330
A confident summary is pleasant.

137
00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.763
The difference is the evidence.

138
00:08:06.263 --> 00:08:09.905
You do not have to read code to judge an
attempt.

139
00:08:09.905 --> 00:08:14.884
Press checks, and Asylum finds your
project's own commands for type

140
00:08:14.884 --> 00:08:19.789
checking, linting, and tests, then runs
them inside that attempt's

141
00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:20.903
private folder.

142
00:08:20.903 --> 00:08:25.660
Passing is real evidence, because it is
your project's own rules

143
00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.518
agreeing with the change.

144
00:08:27.518 --> 00:08:29.375
Failing blocks the merge.

145
00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:33.686
If you are still learning to read code,
treat this as your

146
00:08:33.686 --> 00:08:37.550
health signal first, and read the
difference second.

147
00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:42.745
If an attempt is close but not right, you
do not have

148
00:08:42.745 --> 00:08:43.861
to throw it out.

149
00:08:43.861 --> 00:08:48.531
Click the exact line and write what should
be different in ordinary

150
00:08:48.531 --> 00:08:49.368
language.

151
00:08:49.368 --> 00:08:52.784
Your note is attached to that file and
that line.

152
00:08:52.784 --> 00:08:57.036
Send your notes back and the same
assistant carries on in the

153
00:08:57.036 --> 00:09:00.731
same folder, keeping everything it already
got right.

154
00:09:00.731 --> 00:09:04.774
Review becomes a short conversation rather
than a verdict.

155
00:09:06.274 --> 00:09:11.119
When one attempt is clearly the strongest,
you merge it, which means

156
00:09:11.119 --> 00:09:14.539
folding its changes back into your real
project.

157
00:09:14.539 --> 00:09:19.027
Asylum shows you what is about to happen
before it happens, and

158
00:09:19.027 --> 00:09:21.165
then waits for you to confirm.

159
00:09:21.165 --> 00:09:23.017
Nothing merges on its own.

160
00:09:23.017 --> 00:09:27.506
The attempts you did not keep stay exactly
where they are until

161
00:09:27.506 --> 00:09:32.493
you clear them out, so changing your mind
afterwards costs you nothing

162
00:09:32.493 --> 00:09:33.348
at all.

163
00:09:34.848 --> 00:09:39.815
Now Devpipe, which answers a problem you
meet the moment the work

164
00:09:39.815 --> 00:09:40.732
gets real.

165
00:09:40.732 --> 00:09:42.795
Your project needs secrets.

166
00:09:42.795 --> 00:09:46.922
Keys for services, addresses for
databases, and so on.

167
00:09:46.922 --> 00:09:52.041
The unsafe habit is pasting them into
files and prompts, where they

168
00:09:52.041 --> 00:09:55.174
end up in places you cannot fully recall.

169
00:09:55.174 --> 00:09:57.773
Devpipe is an account level vault.

170
00:09:57.773 --> 00:10:03.198
Asylum reads it through, and never copies
it onto this machine, because

171
00:10:03.198 --> 00:10:08.165
two copies of one secret eventually
disagree about whether it was

172
00:10:08.165 --> 00:10:09.082
deleted.

173
00:10:10.582 --> 00:10:12.705
Entries come in two kinds.

174
00:10:12.705 --> 00:10:18.419
Values, which are ordinary settings, and
secrets, which are protected.

175
00:10:18.419 --> 00:10:20.460
Each one sits at a level.

176
00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.951
Your whole account, a single workspace, or
one machine.

177
00:10:24.951 --> 00:10:29.767
When the same name appears twice, the
narrowest level wins.

178
00:10:29.767 --> 00:10:35.319
And a machine cannot read a secret merely
because the secret exists.

179
00:10:35.319 --> 00:10:38.911
You grant that machine that secret, by
name.

180
00:10:38.911 --> 00:10:42.993
So an assistant can use a key to make a
real call,

181
00:10:42.993 --> 00:10:48.626
without ever being shown the key, and
without getting to choose where

182
00:10:48.626 --> 00:10:49.605
it is sent.

183
00:10:51.105 --> 00:10:55.982
Worth gathering the safety rails in one
place, because they are the

184
00:10:55.982 --> 00:10:58.675
reason you can relax while this runs.

185
00:10:58.675 --> 00:11:02.095
Every attempt is isolated, so nothing
collides.

186
00:11:02.095 --> 00:11:04.716
Nothing is merged unless you say so.

187
00:11:04.716 --> 00:11:09.738
A project cannot run its own setup
commands until you have explicitly

188
00:11:09.738 --> 00:11:10.611
trusted it.

189
00:11:10.611 --> 00:11:13.741
Secrets are resolved outside the
assistant.

190
00:11:13.741 --> 00:11:17.889
And every action leaves a record you can
go back and read

191
00:11:17.889 --> 00:11:18.762
afterwards.

192
00:11:20.262 --> 00:11:22.196
That is the entire loop.

193
00:11:22.196 --> 00:11:23.404
Open a project.

194
00:11:23.404 --> 00:11:28.318
Write one small task with something you
can check at the end.

195
00:11:28.318 --> 00:11:30.734
Give it to a single assistant.

196
00:11:30.734 --> 00:11:32.345
Read the difference.

197
00:11:32.345 --> 00:11:33.553
Run the checks.

198
00:11:33.553 --> 00:11:37.500
Then merge it, or send a note back and let
it try

199
00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:38.467
once more.

200
00:11:38.467 --> 00:11:43.300
Do that one time today, on something small
and low risk, and

201
00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:47.328
every other part of this app will fall
into place.

202
00:11:47.328 --> 00:11:48.939
Thanks for watching.
